WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.840
Authorization models are design tools that help teams organize access decisions.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.620
Role-based access control, or RBAC, assigns permissions through roles.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.800
A role might represent a job function, application responsibility, or operational duty.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:23.340
RBAC can be straightforward to understand and administer, but it can become too coarse if roles are broad or too complex if every exception creates another role.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:33.640
Attribute-based access control, or ABAC, uses attributes such as user properties, resource properties, action type, environment, sensitivity, or context.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:43.860
ABAC can express flexible policies, such as allowing managers to approve requests for their region during business hours or allowing access only when a resource is marked internal.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:49.600
The tradeoff is that policies and attributes must be governed carefully so decisions remain understandable.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:01:01.980
Relationship-based access control, or ReBAC, focuses on relationships between subjects and resources, such as owner, member, parent organization, reviewer, collaborator, or tenant administrator.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.120
Policy-based access concepts separate policy rules from enforcement points.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:14.500
Discretionary and mandatory access concepts at a high level describe who controls access decisions and whether policy is centrally enforced.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:23.680
Groups, roles, permissions, scopes, claims, and entitlements are all ways of representing access information, but none is magic.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:32.140
Choosing a model depends on application complexity, user population, data sensitivity, tenant structure, audit needs, and maintainability.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:37.960
A good model makes the right access easy to express and the wrong access difficult to grant accidentally.
